# Credentials and SDK configuration for the agent-sdk examples.
#
# This file holds one thing: secrets and endpoints - which accounts to talk to.
#
# Everything else is in examples/demo.env, which is git-ignored exactly like this file
# (demo.env.template is the committed copy). That separation is deliberate, and the test for it is
# simple: if you would change a value to play with the examples, it is a fixture and belongs over
# there. A real application knows its tenant, its agent and what it bills against from its own
# code and from the request it is serving - never from an env file.
#
# The SDK's own settings are not variables at all. They are arguments:
#   new StiggAgentSDK(apiKey, baseUrl, { cacheTtlMs, timeoutMs, logger, onError })
#   stigg.wrap(client, { stiggContext: { entitlement, usageMapper, ingestMode, ... } })
# Anything you set here still wins over demo.env, so you can override a fixture without editing it.
#
# Vendor vars are prefixed AGENT_SDK_ so they cannot collide with other packages in this monorepo.
# STIGG_API_KEY and STIGG_BASE_URL are deliberately not: they name the Stigg account this SDK talks
# to, which is the same one every package in the monorepo would mean. There is no AGENT_SDK_ form.
# The vendor API keys also fall back to the bare names (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY /
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY / LITELLM_API_KEY / TYPESAFE_API_KEY / GEMINI_API_KEY / GROQ_API_KEY /
# MISTRAL_API_KEY / OLLAMA_HOST / DATABRICKS_TOKEN / DATABRICKS_HOST), so an already-exported key
# keeps working.

###################################################################################################
# 1. CREDENTIALS & ENDPOINTS - who you are, and where to send requests
###################################################################################################

# --- Stigg -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One key and one URL, pointed at whichever environment you mean. The key is environment-bound, so
# it and the URL always move together - there is no separate profile name to keep in sync.
#
#   production   https://api.stigg.io          (the default when STIGG_BASE_URL is blank)
#   staging      https://api-staging.stigg.io
#   local        http://localhost:4000
#
# This must be a SERVER key (`server-...`): app.stigg.io -> Integrations -> API keys. A publishable
# `client-...` key is frontend-only and every call here fails with `IdentityForbidden`.
# Leave STIGG_API_KEY blank and the examples run against a built-in Stigg mock instead, with no
# account at all - the same rule the vendor keys below already follow.
STIGG_API_KEY=
STIGG_BASE_URL=

# --- Anthropic (client + orchestrator examples) ------------------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://console.anthropic.com -> Settings -> API Keys (see docs/guide.md step 2).
AGENT_SDK_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
AGENT_SDK_ANTHROPIC_MODEL=

# --- OpenAI (client example) -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://platform.openai.com/api-keys.
# Note gpt-5 requires a verified organization; gpt-4.1 does not.
AGENT_SDK_OPENAI_API_KEY=
AGENT_SDK_OPENAI_MODEL=

# --- Gemini (client example) -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey.
AGENT_SDK_GEMINI_API_KEY=
AGENT_SDK_GEMINI_MODEL=

# --- Groq (client example) ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://console.groq.com/keys. The example reaches Groq with the `openai` client
# pointed at https://api.groq.com/openai/v1, which is what Groq's own docs recommend; the binding
# matches on that hostname, so the native `groq-sdk` is governed identically.
AGENT_SDK_GROQ_API_KEY=
AGENT_SDK_GROQ_MODEL=

# --- Mistral (client example) ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://console.mistral.ai/api-keys.
AGENT_SDK_MISTRAL_API_KEY=
AGENT_SDK_MISTRAL_MODEL=

# --- Ollama (client example) -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Self-hosted, so there is NO API KEY - the host is what says "use the real server" here, and with
# it blank the example runs against the built-in mock like every other. Install from
# https://ollama.com, then `ollama pull llama3.3`. Ollama binds 127.0.0.1:11434 by default.
AGENT_SDK_OLLAMA_HOST=
AGENT_SDK_OLLAMA_MODEL=

# --- OpenRouter (client + gateway examples) ----------------------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://openrouter.ai/keys (see docs/guide.md step 3).
AGENT_SDK_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# Primary model, then the fallbacks tried in order. Slugs: https://openrouter.ai/models
AGENT_SDK_OPENROUTER_MODEL=
AGENT_SDK_OPENROUTER_FALLBACK_MODELS=

# --- TypeSafe (client example) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# SDK docs: https://docs.typesafe.ai/sdk/javascript
AGENT_SDK_TYPESAFE_API_KEY=
# The account is served `jev-latest` and `jev-preview`; `client.models.list()` is the roster.
AGENT_SDK_TYPESAFE_MODEL=
# Pins the Wikipedia revision the questions are asked against, so a run is reproducible. Leave
# blank to use the current revision - the id that actually served is printed either way.
AGENT_SDK_TYPESAFE_REVISION=

# --- LiteLLM (gateway example) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# A LiteLLM proxy is self-hosted, so there is no signup - point this at your own deployment.
# Start one locally with:  litellm --model gpt-4o --port 4000
# Leave the key blank and the examples run against the built-in mock instead.
AGENT_SDK_LITELLM_API_KEY=
# Where the proxy is. The default is LiteLLM's own port - note a local Stigg uses 4000 too, so if
# you run both, move one of them.
AGENT_SDK_LITELLM_BASE_URL=
# A model GROUP from the proxy's config, which is free to resolve to a different deployment - that
# difference is the routing drift the examples flag.
AGENT_SDK_LITELLM_MODEL=

# --- Cloudflare Workers AI (client example) ----------------------------------------------------
# A Cloudflare API token with Workers AI read access. Leave it blank for the built-in mock.
AGENT_SDK_CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY=
# Required by every ai.run call - it is part of the URL path, not the request body.
AGENT_SDK_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# A Workers AI model id. Note `@hf/...` ids are Workers AI too, not just `@cf/...`.
AGENT_SDK_CLOUDFLARE_MODEL=

# --- Databricks (client and gateway examples) --------------------------------------------------------------
# A workspace token (a PAT, or an OAuth M2M token - Databricks recommends the latter for anything
# that is not a developer laptop). Leave it blank and the examples run against the built-in mock.
AGENT_SDK_DATABRICKS_API_KEY=
# Your workspace, which is customer-specific and has no default that could possibly be right:
#   AWS    https://<workspace>.cloud.databricks.com
#   Azure  https://adb-<workspace-id>.<n>.azuredatabricks.net
#   GCP    https://<workspace-id>.<n>.gcp.databricks.com
# `/serving-endpoints` is appended if you leave it off. A PrivateLink or custom-DNS workspace works
# too - the recipe is handed this URL, so it does not have to recognise the hostname.
AGENT_SDK_DATABRICKS_BASE_URL=
# A serving ENDPOINT name, not a model id - that distinction is the whole point of this binding.
# Every workspace has the `databricks-*` Foundation Model API endpoints without creating anything;
# an external-model endpoint is one you configured, and its name is whatever you called it.
AGENT_SDK_DATABRICKS_MODEL=
# Set to 1 to assert that the endpoint above is an EXTERNAL model - one carrying your own OpenAI or
# Anthropic key, which that provider invoices rather than Databricks. Nothing on the client side
# distinguishes the two, so this is an assertion about your workspace and the SDK cannot check it.
# It switches both examples to `databricksExternalRecipe()` and the events to
# `databricks-external.*`.
AGENT_SDK_DATABRICKS_EXTERNAL=
# Both Databricks examples read the four names above. `example-client-databricks` is an `OpenAI`
# client on the `/serving-endpoints` route; `example-gateway-databricks` is Databricks' own
# `@databricks/sdk-modelservingquery` client, whose one method is `query`. Same workspace, same
# recipe, two wires - so one set of variables drives both.

# --- Bedrock (gateway example) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no Bedrock key to set here, and that is not an omission. AWS authenticates through its
# own credential chain - AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID/AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_PROFILE, SSO, web identity,
# container and instance roles - and the example asks that chain directly rather than looking for
# a variable. Configure AWS however you already do (`aws configure`, `aws sso login`, a role).
# With nothing the chain can resolve, the example runs against the built-in mock; set
# AGENT_SDK_OFFLINE=1 to force the mock on a machine that does have credentials.
# The region the model is called in. Falls back to AWS_REGION, then us-east-1.
AGENT_SDK_BEDROCK_REGION=
# A Bedrock model id, which names one model exactly - Bedrock does not route, so unlike the
# OpenRouter example there is no fallback list and no drift to report.
# Ids: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/models-supported.html
AGENT_SDK_BEDROCK_MODEL=

###################################################################################################
# 2. DEMO FIXTURES -> see examples/demo.env
###################################################################################################
# Tenant, team, agent, and what the examples gate and meter against - all of it is the demo
# pretending to be an application. It lives in examples/demo.env (git-ignored; demo.env.template
# is the committed reference). Set one here only as a deliberate one-off override: .env is loaded
# first and dotenv never overwrites, so a fixture left here silently shadows demo.env - including
# one you later "fix" over there and wonder why nothing changed.
